1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,787
Eight lectures ago, this section opened with a question nobody can actually check:

2
00:00:04,787 --> 00:00:05,923
is this feature safe.

3
00:00:05,923 --> 00:00:07,222
Here's what replaced it.

4
00:00:07,222 --> 00:00:11,713
You've got a risk register scored the same honest way Section six scored an agent's

5
00:00:11,713 --> 00:00:14,094
blast radius, not a checklist of adjectives.

6
00:00:14,094 --> 00:00:18,422
You know the difference between an injection attempt you typed and one hiding in

7
00:00:18,422 --> 00:00:22,913
content your feature reads, and where the boundary needs to sit between reading and

8
00:00:22,913 --> 00:00:23,292
acting.

9
00:00:23,292 --> 00:00:27,675
You've split "is our data safe" into three separate questions with three separate

10
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:32,220
owners, and you can sort a feature into the EU AI Act's four tiers by what it's used

11
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:33,844
for, not what it's built with.

12
00:00:33,844 --> 00:00:38,389
You've turned "is it biased" into a measured pass-rate gap instead of a feeling, and

13
00:00:38,389 --> 00:00:42,880
you know the difference between a postmortem document and an incident response plan

14
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,315
that actually makes your eval suite stronger.

15
00:00:45,865 --> 00:00:50,353
One downloadable artifact came out of this section, and the workshop put all six

16
00:00:50,353 --> 00:00:55,010
categories through it against a feature of your own: a real attempted attack, not a

17
00:00:55,010 --> 00:00:55,964
hypothetical one.

18
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:57,984
Keep the register alive after today.

19
00:00:57,984 --> 00:01:02,640
It's only useful with a name and a date on every row, revisited, not filled in once

20
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:03,426
and forgotten.

21
00:01:03,426 --> 00:01:08,083
Next section moves from whether a feature is safe to whether it's actually working:

22
00:01:08,083 --> 00:01:12,739
the analytics that tell you if people are getting value, not just using the feature

23
00:01:12,739 --> 00:01:13,805
because it's there.
